message:
{{#is_alert}}The PR agent's p95 cost per review over the last day is over $1.00 — above the single loop's own range (48c–$1.10 per review in the RC1-393 record). Either the multi-agent flag went back off, or a review ran away.{{/is_alert}}
{{#is_warning}}p95 cost per review over the last day is over $0.50 — above the multi-agent path's 18–45c range (RC1-393); worth reading the trace for the most expensive review.{{/is_warning}}

This is the per-*review* signal (RC1-395). The fleet's per-call monitor (RC1-377) measures something else: the multi-agent path replaces one long loop with five or six short calls, so cost per call goes *down* when the flag goes on whether or not the review got cheaper. One `pr_agent.review.cost_usd` point per review, priced at the end of the trace from all four token counts (cache writes 1.25x, reads 0.1x).

Triage on the [fleet dashboard](https://app.datadoghq.com/dashboard/bwm-uny-qqs), "Cost per review" group:
- **by mode**: `mode:single` at 50c–$1.10 is the flag off (`REVIEW_MULTI_AGENT` on the Fly app); `mode:multi` above 50c means the scout ran long — check `scout:ran` and the scout stage on the trace.
- **by repo**: one repo carrying the p95 is a large PR, not a regression.
- The [LLM Obs trace](https://app.datadoghq.com/llm/traces) for the review carries `cost_usd` and `stage_cost_usd.<stage>` on its `pr_review` workflow span.

Thresholds from the RC1-393 corpus run (2026-09-06): multi + 3-turn scout 18–45c, single loop 48c–$1.10. Re-derive when the model or the corpus changes.

